---
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import { projects } from "@/data/projects";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.slug === "nh-small-claims")!;
---

<CaseStudy project={project}>
  <div class="prose-case mt-20">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Small claims court is designed to be usable without a lawyer, but "designed to be" and "actually
      is" are different things. The process, the forms, and the vocabulary are spread across sources that
      quietly assume you already understand how a court works. Someone who has just been served has to
      reverse-engineer the system before they can respond to it.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <p>
      The site is organized around the questions people arrive with rather than the structure of the court
      system: who can file and how, what happens after you're served, which District Division court covers
      your town, how judgments are collected, and how appeals work. Mediation services and free or low-cost
      legal aid are one step away from every page, because for a lot of visitors the right answer is
      talking to a human rather than reading further.
    </p>

    <h2>Impact</h2>
    <p>
      A rotating student volunteer team now maintains a live legal self-help resource covering an entire
      state's small claims process — not a class project sitting unused, but a site people use to work out
      what to do next.
    </p>
  </div>
</CaseStudy>
